,{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"DirectedHypergraph"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A directed hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ordered pair of vertex sets, its sources (tail) and its targets (head). Gallo, Longo, Pallottino"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop (XGI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DiHypergraph"},{"type":"text","text":" allows both)."}],"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","title":"DirectedHypergraph","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"DirectedHypergraph"}]},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph/IncidentHyperedges":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"associatedtype"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"IncidentHyperedges"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Sequence","preciseIdentifier":"s:ST"}],"title":"IncidentHyperedges","required":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph\/IncidentHyperedges","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/directedhypergraph\/incidenthyperedges","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","abstract":[]}}}